Ladybird now grows a line box by the layout bounds of every inline box
that has text or an atomic inline on the line, directly or in a
descendant inline box, and moves the next line past those bounds. It
doesn’t implement the quirks-mode exception the other engines make for
an inline box with no text of its own, though, so it does the same in
quirks mode. Blink computes the text metrics of each inline box as the
box opens, in LogicalLineBuilder::HandleOpenTag(), skipping only empty
boxes in quirks mode. Gecko’s nsLineLayout::VerticalAlignFrames() gives
each span its line-height, and zeroes a span with no real text only in
quirks and almost-standards modes. And WebKit sets the layout bounds of
every inline box in LineBoxBuilder::setLayoutBoundsForInlineBox(),
with InlineQuirks::inlineBoxAffectsLineBox() making the same exception.

Raising the line’s baseline also moves the containing block’s strut,
so its descent still counts below that baseline. The inline boxes’
bounds and the strut both go into the line’s height.
